With the criterion $\{i,p\}\cap\{j,q\}=\varnothing$ from the first solution, count unordered pairs of distinct positions:
* same row ($i=p$, $j\ne q$): choose the row ($n$ ways) and two other columns: $n\binom{n-1}2$;
* same column ($j=q$, $i\ne p$): likewise $n\binom{n-1}2$;
* different rows and columns: choose the $2$ rows, $2$ disjoint columns, and one of $2$ matchings: $2\binom n2\binom{n-2}2$.

Total: $n(n-1)(n-2)+\frac{n(n-1)(n-2)(n-3)}2=\frac{n(n-1)^2(n-2)}2$. Dividing by $\binom{n^2}2=\frac{n^2(n^2-1)}2$ gives $\dfrac{(n-1)(n-2)}{n(n+1)}$.

(Check: $n=3,4,5$ give $\frac16,\frac3{10},\frac25$, matching a computer enumeration.) ∎
